491 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 918 0 R
/E (Bias 1: The Majority Bias \(Bandwagon Effect\): The assumption that whatever the majority of voters choose is inherently "democratic," even if it violates the fundamental rights of a minority.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
492 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 919 0 R
/E (Bias 2: Anchoring Bias: We are anchored to the historical idea that "Democracy = Voting." Therefore, as long as people are casting ballots, we ignore the suppression of rights happening behind the scenes.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
493 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 920 0 R
/E (Logical Fallacy 1: Appeal to Popularity \(Argumentum ad Populum\): "80% of the country voted for this law, so restricting those rights is the democratic will of the people." \(Fact: A democratic process cannot be used to dismantle democratic principles\).)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
494 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 921 0 R
/E (Logical Fallacy 2: False Equivalence: Equating "holding regular elections" with "functioning democracy," completely ignoring that autocracies also hold \(rigged or suppressed\) elections.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [5]
>>
